  health labs that worked  on this testing we work very closely  with the florida tb  program and specifically the project  with duval county health department and  pinellas county health department  thank you and i'm happy to answer any  questions  thank you marie claire for your  informative presentation  we will now start the live q a portion  of the webinar  if you have a question you'd like to ask  please do so now  just click on the ask a question box  located on the far left  of your screen we'll answer as many  questions as we have time for  all right let's go ahead and get started  our first question is  how do you compare igra tests to tv  culture tests and pcr based  tests  agra tests are designed to detect  the cellular immune response to mtb  whereas tb culture tests and  any pcr tests are detecting actual  tb organism in a specimen so  the target of what it is testing is  slightly different  and agra can be used to detect  ltbi so latent tb infection in addition  to  being able to detect active tb disease  when we would use culture and pcr  we're really using that to detect active  tb disease  great thank you okay your next question  what is the florida health department  experience  using serial agra igra excuse me  sampling of individuals whose first  agra test is positive and a second  sample collected within the following  month  is negative  so within the florida department of  health we  sort of try to adhere to the  recommendations and not really perform  serial sampling  in this manner um however it does  sometimes happen that a person  gets two tests within a short period of  time and we get discordant results  and that's really when uh you know the  tb  program and tb medical director get  involved and look at the clinical  picture to determine  the next steps i think we know that with  agra especially with serial  testing you can get test variability  and so so that's why serial sampling is  not really  recommended as well as using agra  to monitor treatment  now your next question has a couple  parts to it and it says at what stage  can a person be infectious regarding tb  besides active tb patients can someone  with latent tb  transmit the microbacterium to others  who come in contact with him  so no with latent tb infection this is  an inactive phase of infection  and therefore  somebody with ltbi is not transmitting  disease however  the challenge with latent tb infection  is that it can progress to active  disease and once it has progressed to  active disease  then that person is capable of  of transmitting tb um and i think that  you know sort of highlights what uh i  was talking about at the beginning of  the presentation  about how we can really prevent a lot of  tb transmission by detecting and  treating  latent tb infections so that those  persons do not progress to  active disease and have the capability  to  transmit  great thank you now are there more  techniques that are being developed to  detect  ltbi one that is affordable so that  developing countries can benefit from  it  so i'm sure there are people working on  techniques to  develop maybe cheaper and and  easier to use rapid diagnostics  but i'm not really involved in  that's what i can't speak to any of that  i do  know that of course the expense  of agro is a drawback of the assay and  of course it  you know it's being done in a laboratory  setting with all the quality  parameters in place so certainly it does  uh add  an expense to the test  do you think tb screenings should be  conducted  on college campuses where students from  all around the world are in close  contact  so my understanding from the  recommendations  is that  widespread screening at college campuses  isn't recommended  um i think the recommendations for  screening  are really looking at persons who are at  high risk um from it coming from  countries where the risk of tb is  increased  um so i like i said i don't think um  it really supports screening of all  students where the majority of them are  going to be  at low risk but certainly identifying  those high risk  persons and getting  them tested would be key  great thank you okay your next question  how were the single tubes kept in the  field  once blood was drawn in the cooler  fridge for example  yes for our single tube drawers we  recommend  collecting and placing in a cooler and  with the projects that we had with  pinellas  county which required them shipping  fedex overnight to us  they shipped on cold packs but that  seemed to  be okay in the the florida heat we  didn't have  any issues with our pre-analytical  part of the assay there so it was a  pretty easy process  thank you now i see this question  popping up quite often  a lot of users want to know if your  slide deck will be available  and it will be so just to let everyone  know in the audience  if you um join the webcast following the  live broadcast come back into the studio  and there will be a link for you to  download the slide  within the studio okay now let's go  ahead and move on  to our next question do you see  many patients who flip flop around the  cutoff  so we have not seen that a lot because  of the  patient population that we have been  testing  based on the um two  initiatives that i mentioned in my  presentation  working with two of our uh county health  departments  so one population there was the  this um shelter card program with the  homeless population and we know that the  homeless population is at greater risk  and then the um the other cohort with  pinellas county  health department was specifically  related to an exposure and a contact  investigation  so the patients we're looking at  are at a higher risk and so we haven't  really seen that flip flop where  people talk about that wobble has been  with  health care providers um that  are tested on some sort of regular basis  and sort of seem to waver around the cut  off and be  positive one time and negative another  but we haven't haven't really seen it  now for this next question it says are  you aware  of an increase of new cases during this  coronavirus pandemic  um if anything we have  actually seen a decrease and  i would say that our  number our volume of samples coming into  the lab  is uh between 50 and 60 percent  of what it was this time last year and  i really think that is because people  are not coming into the tb clinics to  get  tested but we also know that people have  been  um staying at home and staying in  lockdown so it's  time is really going to tell what  impact um covid has on  uh pb testing will there be  persons with tb with a misdiagnosis  because they haven't accessed care  will there be additional transmission  um i i mean i think we don't know the  answer to some of those questions and  it's it's  you know as i said time is going to tell  i what i  obviously you know said at the end of my  presentation is  you know this importance with uh  educating  our healthcare providers to continue to  consider tb specifically in high-risk  populations because it is a resp  in many times with obviously with  pulmonary tb it is a respiratory illness  there can be some similar  symptoms between covid and tb infection  and so maintaining tb on that  differential diagnosis for patients will  be  essential  okay we have time for just a few more  questions and your next question says  will agra be able to detect the immune  complexes  in immunocompromised or immunosuppressed  patients as they are more susceptible to  tb  sorry can you repeat the question of  course  will agra be able to detect the immune  complexes  in immunocompromised or immunosuppressed  patients  so um aigra the current  formulations of agra both the t-spot and  the quantoferon gold class can  detect interferon gamma in both  immune competent  and immune compromised patients so yes  there  are obviously going to be some cases  which i did discuss a little bit in my  presentation of energy uh where you may  get a false negative  in severely immune compromised persons  who  are not eliciting a t cell response at  all  great thank you okay this audience  member wants to know which  pre-analytical system do you use  i'm not sure i'm clear  on the question um  but obviously you know in terms of the  pre-analytical process and i talked  a little bit about the that's where  errors can occur that can lead to  indeterminate rates  um so obviously  following the pre-analytical steps of  the protocol  very clearly is important and that's  we actually monitor our pre-analytical  process  based on our indeterminate rate so our  indeterminate rate really  informs us as to whether the  pre-analytical process  is being performed um  appropriately or not and i did see a  question and i can so i can just answer  it quickly that said what's that  indeterminate rate  and it's below 0.5 percent so it's  really low  um but that also comes with a lot of  outreach and training and because it is  working very closely with our county  health departments  we've been fortunate in that we have  mitigated a lot of those pre-analytical  issues  that can occur  thank you so much marie claire we have  so many great questions  and any questions that we do not get to  today will be addressed via the speaker  um via the contact information that you  provided at the time of registration  so let's go ahead and move on to your  final question did you have one  specifically marie claire here in the  queue that you would like  to answer so just ask the next one  i know we have a lot yeah you could just  ask the next one  okay great all right so let's see  okay would you recommend using qtf  over t-spot assay and why  so i mean i i can't really recommend one  over another i just have our experience  of using the quantiferon  and can just say that we um  like using the quantiferon gold plus  product  um because um we can  automate it in the laboratory um  it talked a little bit about you know we  perform it on  the agility which automates the eia but  now with  options total totally automated options  like the liaison excel  i think there are certainly advantages  to um  the quantifier on assay in those terms  and now with the single tube drawer  that's also  an advantage um that the quantiferon tb  golden tube assay did not have  because the teeth spot was a single draw  and  one of the other um great things about  the plus is having the tb1 and the tb2  tube  now so that it's detecting um  cd4 t-cell and cd8 t-cell  responses so an improved sensitivity to  that assay  thank you so much and marie claire do  you have any final comments  for our audience before we go today  no thank you everyone for your attention  and your questions and any questions  that um  i haven't been able to answer today i'd  be happy to  answer offline by email  thank you again for your time today in  your important work in public health  we would also like to thank lab roots  and our sponsor via foreign for  underwriting today's educational webcast  before we go i'd like to thank the  audience for joining us today and for  their questions  again as a reminder any questions we did  not have time for today  and those that are submitted during the  on-demand period  will be addressed by marie claire via  the contact information you provided at  the time of registration  this webcast can be viewed on demand and  labrets will alert you via email  when it's available for replay we  encourage you to share that email with  your colleagues who may have missed  today's live event  until next time goodbye
 

